while AMD's 400 and 500 series video  cards were great value GPUs they were  aimed at budget gamers this meant that  Nvidia's high-end 10 series cards have  remained unchallenged until now with  AMD's new Vega GPU architecture the  company is back in the enthusiasts mix  and says that its RX Vega 64 can compete  with Nvidia's $550 GeForce GTX 1084 $50  less but is it worth half a grand let's  find out  [Music]  we're reviewing the reference RX Vega 64  which is a dual slot card that measures  ten point five inches and comes with the  traditional black shroud coupled with a  thirty millimeter fan one concern  against it up front is that it's a 295  watt TDP cord this is unprecedented for  any single GPU that we've ever reviewed  and it requires a beefy 750 watt PSU  with to a pin power connectors in terms  of ports the RX Vega 64 includes three  DisplayPort 1.4 connections and one HDMI  output the RX Vega 64 partially gets its  name from the fact that it has 64  compute units in terms of core and boost  clocks its clocked at 12 74 megahertz  and 1546 megahertz respectively aim D  says the card is tuned for high  frequencies and should be able to boost  higher if it gets adequate power and  cooling it uses a gigabytes of second  generation high bandwidth video memory  this new vm technology allows it to  reach up to 483 gigabytes per second of  memory bandwidth that's a lot and should  help it push game textures efficiently  which can come in handy at 4k like  resolution the arcs Vegas 64 is a 12  point 7 teraflop card which trumps  Nvidia's equivalent GPU by 3.8 teraflops  but don't assume that tells the entire  performance story for that let's dig  into the benchmarks since AMD claims  that the RX Vegas 64 is competitive with  Nvidia's GeForce GTX 1080 we're gonna  make direct comparisons against it we  also tested it against the slightly  lower ng TX 1070 and Nvidia's flagship  gtx 980ti to give you additional context  we tested all the cards using the same  system at three resolutions we also ran  each benchmark at their respective necks  settings to really stress test the cards  3d mark 11 extreme is a 1080p test  that's designed to measure GPUs DirectX  11 performance the RX Vegas 64 scored  13% worse than the GTX 1080 here but  spoiler warning AMD's card doesn't  really live up to its full potential at  1080p it seems like the resolution isn't  demanding enough for it to fully flex  its muscles the RX Vegas 64 performs a  bit strangely in the rest of our other  1080p tests it beats the GeForce GTX  1080 in our shadow of mordor and more by  5.5 percent respectively but loses to  the lower tier gtx 1070 in BioShock  Infinite by a respective 10%  the GT X 1080 beats the RX Vega 64 by  8.2% and tomb raider and overall it  seems like the GT x 1080 is better for  standard HD gaming a consolation prize  for AMD's card is that the RX Vegas 64  never dipped below 80 average fps in any  of our 1080p tests this indicates that  it's generally overkill for 1920 by 1080  unless you have a really high refresh  rate monitor moving up to 2560 by 1440  another popular gaming resolution  GeForce GTX 1080 continues to show its  overall edge by repeatedly beating a  Indies card in Tomb Raider BioShock  Infinite and Unigine Valley ambe's card  does once again outperform the GT X 1080  in shadow of mordor by 1.7 percent  respectively though 3840 by 2160 is  where the RX Vegas 64 really starts to  pull ahead ambe's GPU beats the GTX 1080  here in all of our 4k tests oddly enough  one of the biggest leads comes by way of  BioShock Infinite which is where the GTX  1080 really thrashed the RX Vega at the  lower resolution at 4k video Ram can  really bottleneck a GPU since it has to  push so many high resolution assets one  of the likely reasons that the RX Vegas  64 really comes into its own here is  that I can efficiently do so with its  very high memory bandwidth while the RX  Vegas 64 is faster than the GTX 1080 at  4k it's still going to struggle in the  most graphically demanding games like  Deus Ex mankind divided in Valve's V our  benchmark the RX Vegas 64 performed 11%  of worse than the GTX 1080 this suggests  Nvidia's card is better for virtual  reality some people were hoping the  AMD's leading Vega GPU would outperform  the $700 of gtx 980ti  those people may be disappointed with  the RX Vega 64 but it does largely live  up to Andy's claim that it's competitive  with the GTX 280 as a matter of fact it  runs slightly better at 4k given our  results it won't be able to max out  every single 4k game though aim D says  that it's an ideal card to max out the  ultra wide 34 40 by 1440p monitors and  we'd agree and say that's probably worth  sweet sputters while the RX Vegas 64 is  a really good card it's a bit hard to  get super excited when it's coming out  more than 14 months after the GTX 1080  and doesn't completely trounce it it  also consumes a confusing amount of  power still this doesn't prevent the car  from being arguably fast  than the GTX 1084 $50 less the ball is  now an Nvidia score to respond with  either a price drop or a sooner reveal  of its upcoming voltage GPUs because AMD  is back with a compelling choice for  enthusiast gamers for the full in-depth  review check out our written review on  GameSpot calm if you liked this video  make sure you check out our video review  of aim these 500 and 570 GPUs also make  sure to give us a like and subscribe for  more thanks for watching
 

